        <h3>The playmat's corner radius</h3>
        <p class="section-note">
          <strong>Not the Shuffler's 20px/80px.</strong> Those are DOM pixels on a page that
          never zooms; the Tabletop's playmat is a shape on a tldraw canvas the player zooms
          continuously, and a radius fixed in viewport pixels would drift out of proportion to
          the object as the object is resized — Jess: <em>"it shouldn't get rounder as I zoom
          out."</em> A bare CSS percentage doesn't fix this the way it looks like it would
          either: percentage radii use width for the horizontal corner and height for the
          vertical one <em>separately</em>, so on this non-square box it draws an ellipse, not a
          round corner. A real round corner needs one radius, computed from the shape's own
          height and applied equally to both axes — for a self-rendering tldraw shape, that's
          computed from <code>props.h</code> at render time, not a CSS percentage. The two
          candidates below bake that computed value in as px purely so the corner reads as
          actually round here; still open which proportion of height reads right.
        </p>
